Z||000000||Eddie.H||01-05-2000||08:06 PM||||Summit has this stuff called VHT nite-shades lens black out. I just order it. I am going to take the lens covers of and spray the VHT  on the inside of the lens cover. Hay its worth a try right ? (ps part number is vht-sp999)||||reg||6
Z||000001||Cooter||01-05-2000||09:38 PM||||Been there, done that. Got the replacement tail lights for a 73 Camaro to prove it. It was too dark and not enough light came through so I had to replace the lenses. I found that if you find somebody that will custom mix you some paint, get some black single stage enamal and have more binder (clear) added to it until it is thin enough to see through. I used this on VW park and tail lights and it worked great.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Eddie.H||01-05-2000||09:52 PM||||COOTER DID YOU SPRAY it ON THIN just a ? because	I dont want to have to buy new lens covers ps also was it the same VHT stuff. Eddie||||reg||11
Z||000003||Cooter||01-05-2000||10:44 PM||||It has been a few years ago, but I believe that I applied it in two coats. It looked great, but I removed the lenses after a kind officer pointed out that he could not clearly see my brake lights. Try one coat and see how it looks, that may be enough.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Monkeyman||01-05-2000||11:34 PM||||Just a thought, but I wouldn't put any tint on my lights that wasn't easily removed (such as the aftermarket covers).  Some states will fine you if you can't remove the tint immediately.  Besides, you have to make absolutely sure that your tail lights/brake lights are as visible as possible otherwise you're just looking for an accident.  I tried some regular window tint on mine (the lightest shade available) and I could barely see my tail lights from 100 ft.  I took it off immediately.  Not trying to advertise, but if you want a cool alternative to stock tail lights, buy my tail light mod.  It looks good IMHO, is 100% legal (I checked with the Indiana State Police-sent them a pic-got an order!), and is just a visible as the original.||||reg||1
